.TH NOX 1 "2026-09-10" "nox 1.1.0" "NoxCrypt manual"
.SH NAME
nox \- hybrid encryption and signatures from the command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nox
.RI [ global\-option ...]
.I command
.RI [ option ...]
.RI [ operand ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nox
encrypts, decrypts, signs, and verifies files and streams with an
identity that carries both classical and post\-quantum algorithms.
It is deliberately small: no configuration file, no daemon, no
network code, one job per subcommand, and plain files on disk. The
cryptographic primitives come from the vendored libncrypt library
(XChaCha20\-Poly1305, X25519, Ed25519, BLAKE2b, Argon2id, ML\-KEM\-768,
ML\-DSA\-44), so nothing outside the C library has to be installed.
.PP
An identity is a key pair. Its public half lives in a small binary
file whose BLAKE2b\-256 fingerprint identifies it; its secret half is
always locked with a passphrase and never leaves the keyring in the
clear. Every document is either a public key, a secret key, an
encrypted message, or a detached signature, and every document can be
written as raw binary or as PEM\-style ASCII armor.
.PP
Keys are named by a fingerprint prefix of at least eight hex
characters, or by a full 64\-character fingerprint. A comment attached
at generation time is for humans only; it is signed, but nothing
else depends on it.
.SH ALGORITHM PROFILES
An identity holds one or two signing algorithms and one or two
encryption algorithms. The set is chosen when the key pair is created
and is fixed for the life of the key.
.TP
.B hybrid
Ed25519 + ML\-DSA\-44 (signing) and X25519 + ML\-KEM\-768 (encryption).
Both halves have to break for the key to be compromised, which is the
default for a reason. Signatures carry two signature packets and
messages use the combined recipient format.
.TP
.B ecc
Ed25519 + X25519. Small, fast, classical. There is no post\-quantum
protection at all: a future quantum computer can recover the message
key from a recorded ciphertext and forge signatures.
.TP
.B pqc
ML\-DSA\-44 + ML\-KEM\-768. Post\-quantum only, with much larger
signatures and public keys (about 2.4 kB and 1.3 kB) and no classical
fallback.
.TP
.B mixed
Any other valid pair, for example Ed25519 with ML\-KEM\-768. Selected
with
.B \-\-sig
together with
.B \-\-kem
rather than with a profile name.
.PP
ML\-KEM\-768 is a key encapsulation mechanism, not a cipher: it never
encrypts file data itself. The shared secret it produces is hashed
with BLAKE2b into an XChaCha20\-Poly1305 key, and that AEAD key wraps
the random file key. A hybrid recipient does the same, but mixes in an
ephemeral X25519 Diffie\-Hellman result first, so both the classical
and the post\-quantum secret are needed to unwrap.
.PP
Recipient selection follows the key set, not a preference of the
sender. An identity holding both X25519 and ML\-KEM\-768 is always
addressed with the combined recipient, an X25519\-only identity with
the X25519 recipient, an ML\-KEM\-768\-only identity with the ML\-KEM
recipient. A recipient flavor that does not match the identity is
refused in both directions, which is what stops a hybrid key from
being silently downgraded to its classical half.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS gen
.B nox gen
.RI [ \-A
.IR profile ]
.RI [ \-\-sig
.IR alg
.B \-\-kem
.IR alg ]
.RI [ \-c
.IR comment ]
.RI [ \-\-pub
.IR file ]
.RI [ \-\-sec
.IR file ]
.RI [ \-a ]
.RI [ \-F
.IR file ]
.PP
Generates an identity, stores both halves in the keyring, and prints
the fingerprint on stdout. The passphrase is read twice from the
terminal; there is no option to skip it and no way to change it later,
so pick one worth keeping.
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-algo " " \fIprofile\fP
One of
.BR hybrid " (the default), " ecc ", or " pqc .
.TP
.BR \-\-sig " " \fIalg\fP
Signing algorithm for a mixed pair:
.B ed25519
or
.BR mldsa44 .
Must be given together with
.BR \-\-kem .
.TP
.BR \-\-kem " " \fIalg\fP
Encryption algorithm for a mixed pair:
.B x25519
or
.BR mlkem768 .
Must be given together with
.BR \-\-sig .
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-comment " " \fItext\fP
Free\-form label, printed by
.BR "nox list" .
UTF\-8, no line breaks, at most 1024 bytes.
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-pub " " \fIfile\fP
Also write the public key to
.IR file .
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-sec " " \fIfile\fP
Also write the secret key to
.IR file .
It stays locked with the passphrase.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-armor
Armor the files written by
.B \-\-pub
and
.BR \-\-sec .
Files written to a terminal are armored regardless.
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-passphrase\-file " " \fIfile\fP
Read the passphrase from the first line of
.I file
instead of prompting. Meant for scripts and tests.
.SS encrypt
.B nox encrypt
.RI [ \-r
.IR fp ]...
.RI [ \-R
.IR file ]...
.RI | \-p
.RI [ \-a ]
.RI [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RI [ \-F
.IR file ]
.RI [ in ]
.PP
Encrypts
.I in
(or stdin) to one or more recipients and writes a NoxCrypt message.
A message has one random file key, wrapped once per recipient, and a
streaming XChaCha20\-Poly1305 payload; the recipient list is not
secret, the file name and size are not in the file.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-recipient " " \fIfp\fP
A fingerprint prefix from the keyring. Repeat for several recipients.
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-recipient\-file " " \fIfile\fP
A public key file, armored or not. Repeat as needed, also mixed with
.BR \-r .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-passphrase
Encrypt with a passphrase instead of public keys. A passphrase
recipient is always alone; combining it with
.B \-r
or
.B \-R
is an error.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-armor
Armor the message.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIfile\fP
Write to
.I file
instead of stdout.
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-passphrase\-file " " \fIfile\fP
Read the passphrase from
.IR file ;
implies
.BR \-p .
.PP
Binary output to a terminal is refused unless
.B \-a
is given, because a terminal will happily mangle a message into
something that no longer decrypts.
.SS decrypt
.B nox decrypt
.RI [ \-i
.IR fp
| \-p ]
.RI [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RI [ \-F
.IR file ]
.RI [ in ]
.PP
Decrypts a message to stdout or
.IR \-o .
Without
.B \-i
the single secret key in the keyring is used; with several secret
keys, one has to be named. The plaintext is written as it
authenticates, chunk by chunk.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-identity " " \fIfp\fP
Secret key to decrypt with, by fingerprint prefix.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-passphrase
The message has a passphrase recipient.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIfile\fP
Write to
.I file
instead of stdout.
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-passphrase\-file " " \fIfile\fP
Read the passphrase from
.IR file .
.PP
A wrong passphrase, the wrong identity, and a tampered message all
fail with the same kind of error and a non\-zero exit status. Note
that a failure part way through a large file may leave the already
written prefix of the plaintext in place; use
.B \-o
to a temporary file if that matters.
.SS sign
.B nox sign
.RI [ \-i
.IR fp ]
.RI [ \-a ]
.RI [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RI [ \-F
.IR file ]
.RI [ in ]
.PP
Creates a detached signature over
.I in
(or stdin). The message is streamed through BLAKE2b\-256 and never has
to fit in memory. One signature packet is produced for every signing
key of the identity: two for a hybrid identity, one for ecc or pqc.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-identity " " \fIfp\fP
Signing key, by fingerprint prefix.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-armor
Armor the signature.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIfile\fP
Write to
.I file
instead of stdout.
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-passphrase\-file " " \fIfile\fP
Read the passphrase from
.IR file .
.SS verify
.B nox verify
.RI [ \-k
.IR file
| \-i
.IR fp ]
.I signature
.RI [ message ]
.PP
Verifies a detached signature. Without
.B \-k
or
.B \-i
the fingerprint inside the signature selects the key from the keyring.
On success exactly one line is printed, together with exit status 0:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
Good signature
.fi
.RE
.PP
Everything else, including a signature that verifies but is missing
an algorithm the public key demands, prints nothing to stdout and
exits non\-zero.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " " \fIfile\fP
Public key file to verify against, armored or not.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-identity " " \fIfp\fP
Keyring entry to verify against, by fingerprint prefix.
.SS list
.B nox list
.RI [ query ]
.PP
Lists the keyring, one block per key:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
2b7e151628aed2a6abf7158809cf4f3c8e2f...d6e7  2026-09-10
  hybrid: Ed25519 + ML-DSA-44 + X25519 + ML-KEM-768
  "alice@example.com"
.fi
.RE
.PP
The first line is the fingerprint and the creation date in UTC, the
second the profile and its algorithms, the third the comment in
quotes. Keys with no secret half are marked
.BR "(no secret key)" .
Entries are sorted by fingerprint.
.I query
matches a fingerprint prefix or a substring of a comment.
.SS export
.B nox export
.RI [ \-s ]
.RI [ \-a ]
.RI [ \-o
.IR file ]
.I fp
.PP
Writes a key out of the keyring. A public key is what you send to
someone who wants to encrypt to you or verify your signatures.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-secret
Export the secret key instead. The file is still locked with the
passphrase, so it is only as sensitive as the passphrase is.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-armor
Armor the output.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIfile\fP
Write to
.I file
instead of stdout.
.SS import
.B nox import
.RI [ \-F
.IR file ]
.I file
.PP
Reads a public or a secret key file, armored or not, and stores it in
the keyring. A secret key is unlocked first, so a wrong passphrase
fails before anything is written. The fingerprint goes to stdout.
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-passphrase\-file " " \fIfile\fP
Read the passphrase from
.IR file .
.SS delete
.B nox delete
.I fp
.PP
Removes both halves of a key from the keyring. There is no undo and
no confirmation prompt; export the secret key first if you may want it
back.
.SS info
.B nox info
.PP
Prints the version, author, license, the algorithms this build knows,
the cryptographic primitives, and a summary of the keyring. Works
without a keyring.
.SS help
.B nox help
.PP
Prints the short usage summary. Every command also accepts
.BR \-\-help .
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Global options go before the command name and apply to it.
.TP
.BR \-\-home " " \fIdir\fP
Use
.I dir
instead of
.BR $HOME .
The keyring is
.IR dir /.nox
and is created, with parents, mode 0700, if it does not exist.
.TP
.BR \-\-faketime " " \fIseconds\fP
Pretend the clock reads
.I seconds
since the Unix epoch when a timestamp is written. For reproducible
tests, not for dating documents.
.TP
.BR \-\-version
Print
.B nox
and the version number.
.TP
.BR \-\-help
Print the usage summary.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.nox
The keyring, mode 0700. Overridden by
.B $NOX_HOME/.nox
and by
.BR \-\-home .
.TP
.IR fingerprint .pub
Public key, mode 0600. The fingerprint is 64 lowercase hex characters.
.TP
.IR fingerprint .sec
Secret key, mode 0600, always wrapped with Argon2id (t=3, m=65536 KiB,
p=1) and XChaCha20\-Poly1305.
.PP
Files passed on the command line may be raw NoxCrypt documents or
armored, with the
.B "BEGIN NOX ..."
wrapper. Armored input is read leniently: any whitespace inside the
base64 body is ignored and header lines after
.B BEGIN
are skipped.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NOX_HOME
Directory that holds
.IR .nox .
.TP
.B HOME
Used when
.B NOX_HOME
is not set.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Useful failure: bad passphrase, no matching key, a message that does
not authenticate, a malformed file.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: unknown command or option. A missing operand is an
ordinary failure (1), because the command was understood.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a default hybrid identity and list it:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
nox gen \-c 'alice@example.com'
nox list
.fi
.RE
.PP
Create a post\-quantum\-only key and a classical key:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
nox gen \-A pqc \-c 'pqc key'
nox gen \-A ecc \-c 'laptop'
nox gen \-\-sig ed25519 \-\-kem mlkem768 \-c 'mixed pair'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Encrypt a file and put the key where a correspondent can fetch it:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
nox export \-a \-o alice.pub 2b7e1516
nox encrypt \-r 2b7e1516 \-a \-o note.nox.asc note.txt
nox decrypt \-i 2b7e1516 \-o note.txt note.nox.asc
.fi
.RE
.PP
Sign and verify over a pipe:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
tar cf \- src | nox sign \-i 2b7e1516 \-o archive.sig
tar cf \- src | nox verify \-k alice.pub archive.sig
.fi
.RE
.PP
Passphrase\-only encryption, no keys involved:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
nox encrypt \-p \-a \-o backup.asc backup.tar
nox decrypt \-p \-o backup.tar backup.asc
.fi
.RE
.SH SECURITY NOTES
Recipient selection is fail\-closed. A hybrid identity refuses a
single\-algorithm recipient, a signature that omits one of the
identity's signing algorithms is refused, unknown critical packets
abort parsing, and there is no recovery mode that ignores a broken
packet.
.PP
There is no forward secrecy. The file key is wrapped to a long\-term
identity key, so anyone who obtains that secret key later, and kept
the ciphertext, can read it then. The ephemeral X25519 key in a hybrid
recipient belongs to the sender and is thrown away: it saves the
sender from having a long\-term key of its own, but it does not make
old ciphertexts unreadable after the recipient's key leaks.
.PP
Passphrase quality decides the fate of a secret key file, and the
reply to
.BR "\-\-passphrase" .
Argon2id at 64 MiB and three passes makes guessing expensive, not
impossible. A short passphrase protects nothing.
.PP
Timestamps come from the system clock (or
.BR \-\-faketime )
and are covered by the identity self\-signature and by every
signature, but nothing verifies that a date is plausible.
.PP
This implementation has not been audited. The format specification in
.B doc/SPEC.md
describes what the code is supposed to do, not what a third party has
checked.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR age (1),
.BR minisign (1),
.BR gpg (1)
.PP
The format specification and a longer manual ship with the source
tree as
.B doc/SPEC.md
and
.BR doc/manual.md .
